WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
TF-IDF.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.821
Which words actually identify a document?

3
00:00:04.821 --> 00:00:07.067
The most common ones certainly don't.

4
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:11.167
TF-IDF weights each word by two things.

5
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:13.533
How often it appears in this document.

6
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:19.267
Cut by how common it is across every document, so words like the and system fade away.

7
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:24.400
Words distinctive to one document, like cryogenic or hypergolic, pop.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:26.833
Here is the formula.

9
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:30.833
Term frequency is how often the word appears in this document.

10
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:37.840
Inverse document frequency is a log of the ratio: total documents over documents containing the word.

11
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.500
Rarer words score higher.

12
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.100
Let's multiply it out with simple numbers.

13
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.333
The appears five times, but it's in every document, so IDF is zero and the product is zero.

14
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.933
Tank appears twice and is moderately rare: two times point five is one.

15
00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:57.200
Cryogenic appears once but is rare, so it wins.

16
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.333
Now scale this up to a whole corpus.

17
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:02.133
Every document goes in.

18
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:05.267
Each gets weights for every word in the vocabulary.

19
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:10.133
That makes a huge sparse vector, one dimension per word, almost all zeros.

20
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:14.633
Similarity between documents is just the cosine between their vectors.

21
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:18.100
Here's the same pipeline in scikit-learn.

22
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.790
Three tiny documents.

23
00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.833
Two are about the oxygen tank, one is routine.

24
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:27.800
The vectorizer learns the vocabulary and builds the sparse matrix in one call.

25
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.676
Then cosine similarity scores every pair.

26
00:01:30.676 --> 00:01:35.200
The two tank documents score highest, and the routine one is a stranger.

27
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.900
Here's where it earns its keep.

28
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.900
A user asks about hypergolic propellant leaks.

29
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.733
Dense embeddings find similar concepts, but may miss that exact rare term.

30
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:50.933
The sparse leg nails the documents containing it, and fusion merges both lists.

31
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.137
Let's recap.

32
00:01:53.137 --> 00:01:56.733
TF-IDF multiplies term frequency by rarity.

33
00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.733
Common words fade, and distinctive words pop.

34
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:03.133
Documents become sparse vectors you compare with cosine.

35
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:08.514
And its descendant BM25 is still the keyword leg of hybrid search.

36
00:02:08.514 --> 00:02:09.900
Next up: Word2Vec.
